# netsurf: overflow on the root and body goes to the viewport
# Generated 2026-09-24
#
# CSS 2.1 11.1.1: the root element's overflow applies to the viewport, and
# so does the body's when the root's is visible; the element itself then
# behaves as overflow: visible. NetSurf applied body's overflow to the body
# box, so html, body { height: 100% } with body { overflow-y: scroll } --
# Wikipedia's mobile skin -- made the body a scroll box one viewport high
# holding the whole article: extent 1011x544 with an inner scrollbar, and
# the window could not scroll the page at all. Upstream has the same gap.
# - box.h: box flag OVERFLOW_TO_VIEWPORT.
# - box_inspect.h: box_overflow_x/y(), the used overflow (visible for a
#   flagged box, else computed).
# - layout.c: layout_propagate_overflow() at the start of layout_document
#   flags the root, and the <body> child when the root's overflow is
#   visible; every css_computed_overflow_[xy](box->style) in layout.c,
#   redraw.c and box_inspect.c reads box_overflow_[xy](box) instead (the
#   float-dimension helper, given a style rather than a box, is left).
# Verified: the minimal case (X-ovf4) 960x544 -> 960x2280; Wikipedia
# 1011x544 -> 1011x25076, one scrollable page.

